/* 4.0 content: AS and A Level Biology 9700, Topics 1 and 2 */
X4TOPIC("alevel",{
1:["A light microscope can show you a cell. Why can it never show you a ribosome, however strong the lens?","fig:AL_12_17",[["Medicine","Pathologists at University Teaching Hospital diagnose cancers and infections from stained tissue sections and electron micrographs."],["Research","Electron microscopes revealed how viruses such as HIV and SARS-CoV-2 are built, which guided vaccine design."],["Industry","Knowing the structure of bacteria and yeast lets biotechnologists engineer them to make medicines and fuels."]],[1,2,4,5],["How are slides prepared and drawings made accurately?","Why does resolution, not magnification, limit what we can see?","How does the ultrastructure of a cell relate to the jobs of its organelles?"]],
2:["Starch, cellulose and glycogen are all made of glucose. Why can you digest nshima but not the paper it is wrapped in?","fig:AL_22_17",[["Nutrition","Food labs in Lusaka test flour, milk and oils for sugars, proteins and fats using the same tests you will learn."],["Medicine","Sickle cell disease comes from a single change in the primary structure of haemoglobin."],["Climate","Water’s high specific heat capacity keeps Lake Kariba and Lake Tanganyika stable habitats through hot seasons."]],[1,2,3,4],["How do we identify and measure biological molecules?","How are monomers joined and split?","How does the structure of each molecule explain its function?"]]
});
X4ADD("alevel",{
"1.1":{c:["IGCSE cell structure","You have drawn cells before. What makes a biological drawing good enough for an A Level examiner?","1. Name four organelles visible with a light microscope.<br>2. Why do we stain specimens?<br>3. Look at the drawing on the board: list two things wrong with it.",3],
h:["Two learners drew the same onion cells. One drawing scored 5 marks and the other 1. What is the difference?","fig:AL_11_03","The good drawing uses clear, continuous single lines, no shading, correct proportions, ruled label lines that do not cross, and a title with magnification. Biological drawings record what is actually seen, not a textbook diagram."],
a:[["diag","Diagnosis: what went wrong with this slide?",14,"Board only",["Read the three problems on the board, each with a description of what a learner saw.","Slide A: cells overlap in thick clumps, hard to see edges. Slide B: large dark circles with thick black outlines. Slide C: cells colourless and almost invisible.","For each slide, identify the mistake in preparation.","Suggest how to correct each one.","Explain why a thin, single layer of cells is essential."],{ans:["A: specimen too thick or not spread out; use a thinner layer or press gently.","B: air bubbles; lower the coverslip at an angle using a mounted needle.","C: no stain; add iodine (plant cells) or methylene blue (animal cells).","Light must pass through; a single layer lets individual cells and organelles be seen clearly."]}],
 ["prob","Problem solving: mark the drawing",12,"Board only (or a printed drawing)",["Your teacher shows a biological drawing with deliberate mistakes.","In pairs, list every rule it breaks.","Rank the mistakes from most to least serious.","Redraw one part correctly."]]],
p:[["How do we prepare a temporary slide?","sort",2,"Put the steps in order for making a temporary slide of onion epidermis."],
 ["How do we prepare a slide of cheek cells safely?","list",2,"List the steps and one safety precaution for preparing cheek cells."],
 ["Why and how are specimens stained?","comp",2,"Name a stain for plant cells and one for animal cells and what each shows."],
 ["What are the rules for a biological drawing?","list",2,"Write five rules for a good biological drawing."],
 ["How is a plan drawing different from a cell drawing?","comp",2,"Explain when you would use a plan drawing and why it shows no individual cells."]],
s:["Temporary slides use thin specimens, a drop of water or stain and a coverslip lowered at an angle to avoid air bubbles.","Iodine stains starch and nuclei in plant cells; methylene blue stains animal cell nuclei.","Drawings use sharp continuous lines, no shading, ruled labels, correct proportions and a title with magnification.","Plan drawings show tissue layers and their proportions, not individual cells."]},
"1.2":{c:["Lesson 1.1 Slides and drawings","Your drawing is much larger than the real cell. How do you show how much larger, and work back to the real size?","1. Write the magnification formula.<br>2. How many micrometres are in 1 mm?<br>3. Convert 2.5 mm to µm.",3],
h:["An electron micrograph shows a mitochondrion 50 mm long at ×20 000. Is it bigger or smaller than a red blood cell?","fig:AL_11_07","Actual size = 50 mm ÷ 20 000 = 0.0025 mm = 2.5 µm. A red blood cell is about 7 µm, so the mitochondrion is smaller. Always convert to the same units before comparing."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: magnification and scale",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the graticule simulation.","Choose a specimen and record the magnification shown.","Calculate the actual size from the image size.","Check your answer with the simulation’s scale bar."],{sim:"graticule"}],
 ["prob","Problem solving: size detective",14,"Calculator; ruler",["Your teacher gives four micrographs with scale bars or magnifications: a chloroplast, a bacterium, a sperm cell and a starch grain.","Measure each image in mm.","Calculate the actual size in µm.","Put them in order of size. Check: are your answers biologically sensible?"]]],
p:[["How is magnification calculated?","calc",2,"An image is 30 mm and the object is 0.15 mm. Calculate the magnification."],
 ["How do we convert between mm, µm and nm?","calc",2,"Convert 0.004 mm to µm and to nm."],
 ["How do we work out the actual size of a starch grain?","calc",2,"A starch grain image is 24 mm at ×800. Calculate its actual size in µm."],
 ["How do we use a scale bar?","calc",2,"A 10 µm scale bar is 25 mm long. A cell measures 60 mm. Calculate its actual size."],
 ["How do eyepiece and objective lenses give total magnification?","calc",1,"Calculate total magnification for a ×10 eyepiece and ×40 objective."]],
s:["Magnification = image size ÷ actual size.","1 mm = 1000 µm; 1 µm = 1000 nm.","Measure images in mm and convert to µm or nm before comparing.","Scale bar: actual size = (image size ÷ scale bar length) × scale bar value.","Total magnification = eyepiece × objective."]},
"1.3":{c:["Lesson 1.2 Magnification","You can calculate size from a photo. How do you measure a cell while it is actually under your microscope?","1. Write the formula for actual size.<br>2. Convert 0.02 mm to µm.<br>3. Predict: why does a stronger lens not always show more detail?",3],
h:["Why can you magnify a photo on your phone until it is huge, but the picture just becomes blurry squares?","emo:📱 🔍 ▦|Zoom in far enough and the detail stops.","Magnification makes the image bigger; resolution is the ability to distinguish two points that are close together. Once you pass the limit of resolution, extra magnification adds no detail. Light microscopes resolve about 200 nm; electron microscopes about 0.5 nm."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: calibrate the graticule",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the graticule simulation.","Line up the eyepiece graticule with the stage micrometer.","Calculate the value of one eyepiece unit at ×100 and at ×400.","Measure a cell and convert to µm."],{sim:"graticule"}],
 ["data","Data task: calibration practice",12,"Calculator",["Use the data on the board: at ×400, 40 eyepiece divisions line up with 10 stage micrometer divisions; each stage division is 10 µm.","Q1. Calculate the length of one eyepiece division.","Q2. A cell is 22 eyepiece divisions long. Calculate its length.","Q3. Why must you recalibrate when you change objective?"],{ans:["10 × 10 µm = 100 µm over 40 divisions, so 2.5 µm per division.","22 × 2.5 = 55 µm.","Each objective gives a different magnification, so the eyepiece divisions represent different real lengths."]}]],
p:[["What are an eyepiece graticule and a stage micrometer?","comp",1,"Explain why the eyepiece graticule has no units until it is calibrated."],
 ["How is the graticule calibrated?","calc",2,"16 eyepiece units = 4 stage units (each 10 µm). Calculate one eyepiece unit."],
 ["How is a specimen measured with a calibrated graticule?","calc",2,"A cell spans 35 eyepiece units of 2.5 µm. Calculate its size."],
 ["What is the difference between magnification and resolution?","write",2,"Define magnification and resolution in one sentence each."],
 ["How do light and electron microscopes compare?","comp",3,"Make a table comparing light, TEM and SEM: radiation used, resolution, maximum magnification, living specimens?"]],
s:["An eyepiece graticule is calibrated against a stage micrometer for each objective.","Value of one eyepiece unit = stage length ÷ number of eyepiece units.","Resolution is the ability to distinguish two close points; it depends on the wavelength used.","Electron microscopes have much higher resolution (about 0.5 nm) than light microscopes (about 200 nm) because electrons have a shorter wavelength."]},
"1.4":{c:["Lesson 1.3 Resolution","Electron microscopes reveal structures inside organelles. Which organelles are bounded by membranes, and why does that matter?","1. Give the resolution of a light microscope.<br>2. Name two organelles only visible with an electron microscope.<br>3. Predict: why do cells need internal membranes?",3],
h:["Why do mitochondria and chloroplasts have their own DNA and ribosomes, like tiny bacteria inside our cells?","fig:AL_12_03","Mitochondria and chloroplasts have double membranes, circular DNA and 70S ribosomes. The endosymbiont theory suggests they were once free-living prokaryotes taken in by an early eukaryotic cell."],
a:[["cards","Card activity: organelle and function",15,"Printed cards (1 set per group)",["Lay out the organelle cards and the function cards.","Match each organelle to its function.","Sort the organelles into: double membrane, single membrane, no membrane.","Check with the notes and another group."],{cards:[["Nucleus","Contains DNA; controls the cell through transcription"],["Nucleolus","Makes ribosomes (rRNA)"],["Mitochondrion","Site of aerobic respiration; makes ATP"],["Chloroplast","Site of photosynthesis"],["Ribosome (80S)","Site of protein synthesis in the cytoplasm"],["Cell surface membrane","Controls entry and exit; cell signalling"],["Cell wall","Strengthens; resists osmotic bursting"]],pairs:1}],
 ["disc","Discussion: evidence for endosymbiosis",10,"None",["List the features shared by mitochondria and bacteria.","Pairs decide which feature is the strongest evidence and why.","Share: what other evidence would convince you?"]]],
p:[["What is the structure and role of the cell surface membrane?","sketch",2,"Sketch a membrane section and label the phospholipid bilayer."],
 ["What is the cell wall made of and what does it do?","comp",1,"Compare the cell walls of plants, fungi and bacteria."],
 ["What does the nucleus contain and do?","label",2,"Label the nuclear envelope, nuclear pores, chromatin and nucleolus."],
 ["How is the mitochondrion adapted for respiration?","expl",2,"Explain why the inner membrane is folded into cristae."],
 ["How is the chloroplast adapted for photosynthesis?","label",2,"Label the chloroplast: envelope, grana, thylakoids, stroma, starch grain, DNA, ribosomes."],
 ["What are ribosomes and where are they found?","comp",1,"Compare 70S and 80S ribosomes and where each is found."]],
s:["The cell surface membrane is a partially permeable phospholipid bilayer.","Plant cell walls are cellulose; fungal walls chitin; bacterial walls peptidoglycan.","The nucleus has a double envelope with pores, chromatin and a nucleolus.","Mitochondria (cristae, matrix) and chloroplasts (thylakoids, stroma) have double membranes, circular DNA and 70S ribosomes.","80S ribosomes are found in the cytoplasm and on rough ER of eukaryotes."]},
"1.5":{c:["Lesson 1.4 Organelles","Ribosomes make proteins. Many proteins are sent out of the cell. What route do they take?","1. What is made at ribosomes?<br>2. Which organelle has cristae?<br>3. Predict: a cell that secretes enzymes would have lots of which organelles?",3],
h:["A pancreas cell makes and secretes enzymes all day. What would you expect to see when you look at it with an electron microscope?","fig:AL_12_08","Lots of rough ER (ribosomes making proteins), a large Golgi apparatus (modifying and packaging them), many vesicles (carrying them to the membrane) and many mitochondria (supplying ATP). Structure reflects function."],
a:[["role","Role play: the protein production line",12,"Labels for each organelle (paper)",["Learners take roles: nucleus, mRNA, ribosome on RER, RER lumen, transport vesicle, Golgi, secretory vesicle, cell membrane.","A ‘protein’ (paper strip) is made and passed along the line.","At each stage the learner says what happens to the protein.","Run it again showing a lysosomal enzyme going to a lysosome instead.","Discuss: which step needs ATP?"]],
 ["prob","Problem solving: name the cell",12,"Board only",["Your teacher describes three cells by their organelles: A has huge amounts of RER and Golgi; B has many mitochondria and microvilli; C has many lysosomes.","Suggest a cell type for each.","Explain how each organelle fits the cell’s job."]]],
p:[["What do rough and smooth ER do?","comp",2,"Compare rough and smooth ER: structure and function."],
 ["What does the Golgi apparatus do?","sort",2,"Put in order: protein made on RER, vesicle buds off, fuses with Golgi, modified, packaged, secreted."],
 ["What are vacuoles, vesicles and lysosomes?","comp",2,"Explain what a lysosome contains and one use."],
 ["What do centrioles do?","think",1,"Where are centrioles found and what role do they play in cell division?"],
 ["What is the cytoskeleton and what does it do?","list",2,"List three functions of microtubules."],
 ["How are microvilli, cilia and flagella different?","comp",2,"Make a table: structure and function of microvilli, cilia and flagella."]],
s:["Rough ER (with ribosomes) makes and transports proteins; smooth ER makes lipids and steroids.","The Golgi modifies proteins (for example adding carbohydrate) and packages them in vesicles.","Lysosomes contain hydrolytic enzymes; vesicles transport substances.","Centrioles (in animal cells) organise microtubules for the spindle.","Microtubules form the cytoskeleton; cilia and flagella move; microvilli increase surface area."]},
"1.6":{c:["Lesson 1.5 ER, Golgi and cytoskeleton","You now know the organelles. Can you tell an animal cell from a plant cell in an electron micrograph, and what powers all their work?","1. Name the organelle that packages proteins.<br>2. What do lysosomes contain?<br>3. Predict: name the molecule that is the cell’s ‘energy currency’.",3],
h:["An electron micrograph shows a cell with a large central vacuole, chloroplasts and a thick wall. Another has centrioles and microvilli. Which is which?","fig:AL_12_18","The first is a plant cell; the second an animal cell. Both use ATP as the universal energy currency: ATP is made in respiration and hydrolysed to release energy for active transport, synthesis and movement."],
a:[["prob","Problem solving: identify the micrographs",14,"Board only",["Your teacher shows three electron micrographs labelled A, B, C.","For each, list the organelles you can identify.","Decide: plant cell, animal cell or prokaryote. Give three pieces of evidence.","Estimate the actual size of one organelle using the scale bar."]],
 ["disc","Discussion: why ATP and not glucose?",8,"None",["Think, pair, share: why do cells not use glucose directly to power reactions?","List two advantages of ATP.","Share and compare."]]],
p:[["How do we interpret electron micrographs?","apply",2,"Identify three organelles in the micrograph on the board and give one feature you used for each."],
 ["How do animal and plant cells compare?","comp",2,"List four structures in plant cells absent from animal cells, and one in animal cells absent from plant cells."],
 ["What does an animal cell look like at ultrastructure level?","label",2,"Label an electron micrograph or diagram of an animal cell with ten structures."],
 ["What does a plant cell look like at ultrastructure level?","label",2,"Label a plant cell including chloroplasts, tonoplast, plasmodesmata and cell wall."],
 ["Why is ATP described as the universal energy currency?","expl",2,"Give three reasons ATP is a good energy currency."]],
s:["Plant cells have cell walls, chloroplasts, a large permanent vacuole with tonoplast and plasmodesmata; animal cells have centrioles and sometimes microvilli.","Electron micrographs show ultrastructure; scale bars allow sizes to be calculated.","ATP is made in respiration and hydrolysed to ADP and phosphate, releasing small, manageable amounts of energy.","ATP is used for active transport, synthesis, movement and cell division."]},
"1.7":{c:["Lesson 1.6 Cells and ATP","All the cells so far are eukaryotic. Most living things on Earth are much simpler. How are they built?","1. Name two organelles found in both plant and animal cells.<br>2. What size are ribosomes in eukaryotic cytoplasm?<br>3. Predict: name two differences between bacteria and your cells.",3],
h:["Antibiotics kill bacteria in your blood but do not harm your own cells. How can a drug tell the difference?","fig:AL_12_21","Bacteria are prokaryotes with structures we do not have: a peptidoglycan cell wall and 70S ribosomes. Many antibiotics target these. Viruses have none of these structures, which is why antibiotics do not work on them."],
a:[["cards","Card activity: prokaryote, eukaryote or virus?",15,"Printed cards (1 set per group)",["Lay out the three heading cards.","Sort each feature card under every heading it applies to (some go under two).","Agree one feature that best separates each pair of groups.","Check with another group."],{cards:[["Circular DNA","Prokaryote"],["70S ribosomes","Prokaryote"],["Peptidoglycan cell wall","Prokaryote"],["Plasmids","Prokaryote"],["Linear DNA in a nucleus","Eukaryote"],["80S ribosomes","Eukaryote"],["Membrane-bound organelles","Eukaryote"],["Protein coat (capsid)","Virus"],["Not made of cells","Virus"],["Reproduces only inside a host cell","Virus"]],cats:["Prokaryote","Eukaryote","Virus"]}],
 ["case","Case study: HIV and antiretroviral drugs",12,"Board only",["Read the case on the board.","Q1. Name two structures of HIV.","Q2. Why are antibiotics useless against HIV?","Q3. How do antiretroviral drugs work, in general terms?"],{study:"HIV is a virus with an RNA genome, a protein capsid and an outer envelope from the host cell membrane, with glycoproteins that attach to T-helper cells. Once inside, it uses an enzyme, reverse transcriptase, to make DNA from its RNA. Antiretroviral drugs used in Zambia block viral enzymes such as reverse transcriptase, stopping the virus multiplying.",src:"Based on WHO and NIH information on HIV structure and antiretroviral therapy",ans:["RNA, capsid, envelope, glycoproteins, reverse transcriptase.","Viruses have no cell wall, ribosomes or metabolism for antibiotics to target.","They inhibit viral enzymes (for example reverse transcriptase), so the virus cannot replicate."]}]],
p:[["What are the structural features of prokaryotic cells?","label",2,"Draw and label a typical bacterial cell with eight features."],
 ["How do prokaryotes and eukaryotes compare?","comp",3,"Make a table comparing size, DNA, ribosomes, organelles and cell wall."],
 ["What are the key features of viruses?","list",2,"List the key features of a virus and explain why viruses are not cells."]],
s:["Prokaryotes are small (about 1–5 µm), with circular DNA, 70S ribosomes, a peptidoglycan wall and no membrane-bound organelles.","Eukaryotes have a nucleus, linear DNA with histones, 80S ribosomes and membrane-bound organelles.","Viruses are non-cellular: nucleic acid (DNA or RNA) inside a protein capsid, sometimes with an envelope."]},
"2.1":{c:["Topic 1 Cell structure","Cells are built from carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids. How do we show which ones are present in a sample?","1. Name the four main groups of biological molecules.<br>2. Recall the IGCSE food tests for starch and protein.<br>3. Predict: why do we heat Benedict’s solution?",3],
h:["A sweet drink tests negative with Benedict’s solution, yet it tastes very sweet. What sugar could it contain?","emo:🥤 🧪 🔵|Sweet taste. Benedict’s stays blue.","It probably contains sucrose, a non-reducing sugar. Boiling with dilute acid hydrolyses sucrose into glucose and fructose, which then give a positive Benedict’s test (brick-red precipitate) after neutralising."],
a:[["diag","Diagnosis: identify the unknown solutions",15,"Board only",["Read the results table on the board for five unknown solutions, A to E, tested with iodine, Benedict’s, biuret, emulsion and the non-reducing sugar test.","Identify what each solution contains.","Explain any result that needed two steps.","Suggest a control for each test."],{study:"A: iodine blue-black; others negative. B: Benedict’s brick red; others negative. C: biuret purple; others negative. D: Benedict’s blue; after boiling with acid and neutralising, Benedict’s brick red. E: emulsion test white cloudy layer; biuret purple.",src:"Illustrative results",ans:["A starch; B reducing sugar; C protein; D non-reducing sugar (for example sucrose); E lipid and protein (for example milk).","D needed acid hydrolysis to break glycosidic bonds into reducing sugars.","Repeat each test with distilled water instead of the sample."]}],
 ["disc","Discussion: which test, and why?",8,"None",["The teacher names five foods. Pairs predict which tests would be positive for each.","Share and justify using what each food is made of."]]],
p:[["Which tests identify the key biological molecules?","list",2,"Make a table of the four standard tests: reagent, method, positive result."],
 ["How does the Benedict’s test work?","expl",2,"Explain why heating is needed and why the colour can range from green to brick red."],
 ["How does the iodine test work?","think",1,"What colour shows starch and why does the test work only with starch?"],
 ["How does the emulsion test work?","sort",2,"Write the steps of the emulsion test in order."],
 ["How does the biuret test work?","think",1,"Which bond does biuret react with?"],
 ["How do we test for non-reducing sugars?","sort",2,"Put in order: negative Benedict’s, boil with acid, neutralise, Benedict’s again, positive result."]],
s:["Benedict’s: heat with sample; brick-red precipitate shows reducing sugar.","Iodine in potassium iodide: blue-black with starch.","Emulsion test: shake with ethanol, add water; milky white emulsion shows lipid.","Biuret: lilac/purple with peptide bonds (protein).","Non-reducing sugars: negative Benedict’s, then hydrolyse with acid, neutralise and retest."]},
"2.2":{c:["Lesson 2.1 Tests for biological molecules","The Benedict’s test shows whether reducing sugar is present. Can it show how much?","1. What colour is a strong positive Benedict’s result?<br>2. What does a colorimeter measure?<br>3. Predict: how would you make a 0.5% solution from a 1% solution?",3],
h:["Five Benedict’s tubes go from blue to green to yellow to orange to brick red. Can we turn colours into numbers?","fig:AL_21_03","Yes. A semi-quantitative test compares colours with standards of known concentration. A colorimeter goes further: it measures the absorbance or transmission of light, and a calibration curve converts the reading into a concentration."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: the colorimeter",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the colorimeter simulation.","Make a set of standard glucose solutions by serial dilution.","Measure the absorbance of each and plot a calibration curve.","Use the curve to find the concentration of an unknown sample."],{sim:"colorimeter"}],
 ["data","Data task: build a calibration curve",14,"Graph paper; calculator",["Use the data on the board: absorbance after Benedict’s test for glucose standards.","Plot absorbance against concentration and draw a line or curve of best fit.","Read off the concentration of two unknown samples.","Explain why standards must be tested in exactly the same way as the unknowns."],{study:"Standards (mmol/dm³): 0 → absorbance 0.02; 2 → 0.18; 4 → 0.35; 6 → 0.51; 8 → 0.66; 10 → 0.78. Unknown X: 0.42. Unknown Y: 0.70.",src:"Illustrative results",ans:["X ≈ 4.9 mmol/dm³; Y ≈ 8.6 mmol/dm³ (read from the graph).","Same volumes, same heating time and temperature, same filter, so only concentration affects the reading."]}]],
p:[["How can the Benedict’s test be made semi-quantitative?","list",2,"List three variables to control when comparing Benedict’s colours."],
 ["What alternatives are there to waiting a fixed time?","think",2,"Explain how measuring the time to the first colour change can compare concentrations."],
 ["How do we make a serial dilution?","calc",2,"Describe how to make 10 cm³ each of 1%, 0.1% and 0.01% glucose from a 10% solution."],
 ["How does a colorimeter work?","sort",2,"Put in order: zero with blank, select filter, place cuvette, read absorbance."],
 ["How do we use a calibration curve?","apply",2,"Explain how you would use a calibration curve to find an unknown glucose concentration."]],
s:["Semi-quantitative Benedict’s compares colours (or time to colour change) with known standards.","Serial dilutions reduce concentration by the same factor each step.","A colorimeter measures absorbance or transmission of light through a sample.","A calibration curve of known concentrations is used to read off unknown concentrations."]},
"2.3":{c:["Lesson 2.2 Colorimetry","Glucose gives a positive Benedict’s test. What is glucose actually like, and how do small molecules build big ones?","1. Name a reducing sugar.<br>2. What does a calibration curve show?<br>3. Predict: why might two forms of the same sugar behave differently?",3],
h:["Starch and cellulose are both made only of glucose. Why are they so different?","fig:AL_22_03","The difference lies in α-glucose and β-glucose, which differ in the position of the –OH group on carbon 1. Starch is made of α-glucose and coils; cellulose is made of β-glucose and forms straight chains that bond into strong fibres."],
a:[["model","Model it: build glucose and its polymers",15,"Paper templates of glucose (or coloured paper and scissors)",["Each learner draws or cuts out an α-glucose ring with numbered carbons.","Pairs flip one ring to make β-glucose: note the –OH on carbon 1.","Groups join α-glucose rings into a short chain; another group joins β-glucose rings, flipping alternate rings.","Compare the shapes of the two chains.","Explain which chain could form straight fibres."]],
 ["disc","Discussion: why carbon?",8,"None",["Think, pair, share: why is life based on carbon and not another element?","List two properties of carbon that matter.","Share and compare with silicon."]]],
p:[["What do the key terms monomer, polymer and macromolecule mean?","write",2,"Define monomer, polymer and macromolecule with an example of each."],
 ["Why is carbon so important?","list",1,"Give two reasons carbon forms the backbone of biological molecules."],
 ["How are monomers, polymers and macromolecules related?","sort",2,"Match: amino acids, nucleotides, monosaccharides → proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides."],
 ["How are carbohydrates classified?","comp",2,"Give an example of a monosaccharide, disaccharide and polysaccharide, with a function."],
 ["How do α-glucose and β-glucose differ?","sketch",2,"Draw α- and β-glucose and circle the difference."]],
s:["Monomers join to form polymers; macromolecules are large molecules such as proteins and nucleic acids.","Carbon forms four covalent bonds and stable chains and rings.","Carbohydrates are monosaccharides, disaccharides and polysaccharides.","α-glucose and β-glucose differ in the position of the H and OH on carbon 1."]},
"2.4":{c:["Lesson 2.3 Monomers and polymers","Monomers join to make polymers. What reaction joins them, and what reaction splits them?","1. Name the two forms of glucose.<br>2. What is a polymer?<br>3. Predict: when two molecules join, what small molecule might be released?",3],
h:["When you chew bread for a long time, it starts to taste sweet. What is happening chemically?","emo:🍞 👅 🍬|Plain bread, chewed for a minute, tastes sweet.","Amylase in saliva hydrolyses starch: water is used to break glycosidic bonds, releasing maltose, which tastes sweet. Building polymers releases water (condensation); breaking them uses water (hydrolysis)."],
a:[["model","Model it: condensation and hydrolysis with paper",12,"Paper strips with –H and –OH ends; scissors; tape",["Each learner has a monomer strip with an –H on one end and an –OH on the other.","To join two strips, cut off –OH from one and –H from the other: these make a ‘water’ molecule.","Build a chain of five. Count the water molecules released.","Reverse it: add a water to break one bond.","Write the general rule for n monomers."]],
 ["prob","Problem solving: count the water",10,"Board only",["A polysaccharide has 500 glucose units in one chain.","Q1. How many glycosidic bonds does it have?","Q2. How many water molecules were released making it?","Q3. How many water molecules are needed to hydrolyse it completely?"],{ans:["499.","499.","499."]}]],
p:[["What kind of bonds join monomers into polymers?","think",1,"Name the type of bond holding monomers together and why it is strong."],
 ["What is a condensation reaction?","sketch",2,"Draw two glucose molecules joining and show where the water comes from."],
 ["What is a hydrolysis reaction?","write",2,"Write a general equation for hydrolysis of a dimer."],
 ["Which covalent bonds are found in organic molecules?","comp",2,"Name the bond formed in each: carbohydrates, proteins, triglycerides."]],
s:["Monomers are joined by covalent bonds in condensation reactions, releasing water.","Hydrolysis uses water to break covalent bonds between monomers.","Glycosidic bonds join monosaccharides; peptide bonds join amino acids; ester bonds join fatty acids to glycerol.","n monomers form a chain with n − 1 bonds."]},
"2.5":{c:["Lesson 2.4 Condensation and hydrolysis","Disaccharides are formed by condensation. Why does maltose give a positive Benedict’s test but sucrose does not?","1. What is released in a condensation reaction?<br>2. Name the bond between two monosaccharides.<br>3. Predict: which part of a sugar reacts with Benedict’s solution?",3],
h:["Why is table sugar (sucrose) a non-reducing sugar, while maltose and glucose are reducing sugars?","fig:AL_22_11","Reducing sugars have a free aldehyde or ketone group that can donate electrons to copper(II) ions in Benedict’s solution. In sucrose, the glycosidic bond joins carbon 1 of glucose to carbon 2 of fructose, so both reducing groups are used up."],
a:[["prob","Problem solving: name the disaccharide",12,"Board only",["Your teacher gives three ‘mystery sugar’ descriptions: monomers and Benedict’s result.","Identify each disaccharide (maltose, sucrose, lactose).","Write the condensation equation for each.","Explain which bond types are present."]],
 ["disc","Discussion: lactose intolerance",8,"None",["Many adults in Zambia and worldwide feel unwell after drinking fresh milk.","Pairs explain what lactose is made of and which enzyme breaks it down.","Share: why does fermented milk (mabisi) cause fewer problems?"]]],
p:[["What are reducing and non-reducing sugars?","comp",2,"Classify glucose, fructose, maltose, lactose, sucrose as reducing or non-reducing."],
 ["What is a glycosidic bond?","sketch",2,"Draw the 1,4 glycosidic bond between two α-glucose molecules."],
 ["Where are glycosidic bonds in maltose, sucrose and polysaccharides?","comp",2,"Name the monomers and bond type in maltose, sucrose and lactose."],
 ["How are glycosidic bonds broken?","list",2,"Give two ways glycosidic bonds can be broken in a lab or in the body."]],
s:["Reducing sugars (all monosaccharides, maltose, lactose) reduce copper(II) ions in Benedict’s solution; sucrose does not.","A glycosidic bond forms by condensation between two monosaccharides.","Maltose = glucose + glucose; sucrose = glucose + fructose; lactose = glucose + galactose.","Glycosidic bonds are broken by hydrolysis, using acid and heat or enzymes."]},
"2.6":{c:["Lesson 2.5 Glycosidic bonds","Glucose units can be joined in different ways. How do the shapes of starch, glycogen and cellulose suit their jobs?","1. Name the bond between glucose units.<br>2. Is starch made of α- or β-glucose?<br>3. Predict: why do animals store glycogen, not starch?",3],
h:["A cassava root, a goat’s liver and a cotton shirt are all made largely of glucose polymers. Why are they so different?","fig:AL_22_19","Cassava stores starch (amylose coils and branched amylopectin) as compact energy stores. Liver stores glycogen, which is even more branched so it can be hydrolysed quickly. Cotton is cellulose: straight β-glucose chains hydrogen-bonded into strong microfibrils."],
a:[["cards","Card activity: starch, glycogen or cellulose?",15,"Printed cards (1 set per group)",["Lay out the three heading cards.","Sort the feature cards under the right heading (some fit two).","Write one sentence linking a structural feature to its function for each polymer.","Check with another group."],{cards:[["α-glucose monomers","Starch"],["Amylose: unbranched helix","Starch"],["Amylopectin: some 1,6 branches","Starch"],["Very highly branched","Glycogen"],["Stored in liver and muscle","Glycogen"],["Many ends for rapid hydrolysis","Glycogen"],["β-glucose monomers","Cellulose"],["Alternate glucose units flipped","Cellulose"],["Straight chains with hydrogen bonds between them","Cellulose"],["Forms microfibrils with high tensile strength","Cellulose"]],cats:["Starch","Glycogen","Cellulose"]}],
 ["disc","Discussion: why can’t we digest cellulose?",8,"None",["Goats and cattle digest grass, but humans cannot digest cellulose. Why?","Pairs explain using enzymes and bond shapes.","Share: how do cattle manage it?"]]],
p:[["How do the structures of starch and glycogen suit them as stores?","comp",2,"List three features that make starch and glycogen good energy stores."],
 ["How are amylose and amylopectin different?","comp",2,"Compare amylose and amylopectin: bonds, branching, shape."],
 ["Why is glycogen so highly branched?","expl",2,"Explain how branching helps animals release glucose quickly."],
 ["How is cellulose built?","sketch",2,"Draw two β-glucose units joined, showing the flipped orientation."],
 ["How does cellulose’s structure give strength?","sort",2,"Put in order: β-glucose chains, hydrogen bonds, microfibrils, fibres, cell wall."]],
s:["Starch (plants) and glycogen (animals) are compact, insoluble stores of α-glucose that do not affect water potential.","Amylose is an unbranched helix with 1,4 bonds; amylopectin and glycogen have 1,4 and 1,6 bonds and branch.","Glycogen is more branched, so it is hydrolysed faster.","Cellulose is made of β-glucose; straight chains hydrogen-bond into microfibrils with high tensile strength."]},
"2.7":{c:["Lesson 2.6 Polysaccharides","Starch is a good energy store. Fat stores more than twice as much energy per gram. Why?","1. What is glycogen?<br>2. Which elements are in carbohydrates?<br>3. Predict: why do hibernating animals store fat, not glycogen?",3],
h:["A camel’s hump is mostly fat, not water. Why is that useful in the desert?","emo:🐫 ☀️|Days without food or water.","Triglycerides store more energy per gram than carbohydrates, and when they are respired they release a lot of metabolic water. Fat also insulates and is light for the energy it holds."],
a:[["data","Data task: energy in food stores",12,"Board only",["Use the data on the board: energy per gram of carbohydrate, lipid and protein; water released per gram respired.","Q1. Calculate how many grams of glycogen would store the same energy as 10 g of fat.","Q2. Explain why migrating birds store fat.","Q3. Explain why fat stores help desert animals."],{study:"Energy released when respired: carbohydrate 17 kJ/g; lipid 39 kJ/g; protein 17 kJ/g. Metabolic water: carbohydrate 0.6 g per g; lipid 1.1 g per g.",src:"Typical textbook values",ans:["10 × 39 ÷ 17 ≈ 23 g of glycogen.","Lipid stores twice as much energy per gram, so they carry less mass for long flights.","Respiring lipid gives much more metabolic water."]}],
 ["model","Model it: build a triglyceride",12,"Paper templates of glycerol and fatty acids; scissors; tape",["Each group has one glycerol and three fatty acid strips.","Join each fatty acid to glycerol by removing –H and –OH: an ester bond forms each time.","Count the water molecules released.","Bend one fatty acid strip to show a C=C double bond (unsaturated). How does it change packing?"]]],
p:[["What are lipids?","list",1,"Give three properties shared by lipids."],
 ["How are fatty acids saturated or unsaturated?","sketch",2,"Draw a short saturated fatty acid and an unsaturated one, and circle the difference."],
 ["How does esterification form a triglyceride?","write",2,"Write a word equation for making a triglyceride and state the number of water molecules released."],
 ["How does the structure of a triglyceride relate to its functions?","comp",3,"Link three features of triglycerides to three functions."]],
s:["Lipids are insoluble in water and soluble in organic solvents.","Saturated fatty acids have no C=C bonds; unsaturated have one or more, causing kinks.","A triglyceride forms when three fatty acids join to glycerol by ester bonds in condensation reactions.","Triglycerides are energy stores, insulators, buoyant and a source of metabolic water."]},
"2.8":{c:["Lesson 2.7 Triglycerides","Replace one fatty acid in a triglyceride with a phosphate group, and you get a molecule that builds every membrane. Why?","1. Name the bond between fatty acid and glycerol.<br>2. Are triglycerides soluble in water?<br>3. Predict: what does ‘hydrophilic’ mean?",3],
h:["Shake oil and water and they separate. Add a little washing-up liquid and they mix. What is it doing?","emo:🫗 💧 🧴|Oil and water don’t mix. Add soap, and they do.","Soap molecules have a hydrophilic head and a hydrophobic tail, so they sit between oil and water. Phospholipids are similar: their hydrophilic phosphate heads face water and their hydrophobic fatty acid tails face inwards, forming a bilayer."],
a:[["role","Role play: be a phospholipid bilayer",10,"Open space",["Each learner is a phospholipid: head (your head) loves water; legs (two tails) hate water.","Stand in a ‘water’ area. Arrange yourselves so no legs touch water.","Discover the bilayer: two rows with legs inwards.","Try to let a ‘water molecule’ (a small learner) through. What happens?"]],
 ["disc","Discussion: phospholipid versus triglyceride",8,"None",["Pairs list two similarities and three differences.","Share: why can a triglyceride not form a membrane?"]]],
p:[["What is the structure of a phospholipid?","sketch",2,"Draw a phospholipid and label the hydrophilic head and hydrophobic tails."],
 ["Why do phospholipids form membranes?","expl",2,"Explain why phospholipids form a bilayer in water."],
 ["How do phospholipids and triglycerides compare?","comp",2,"Make a table comparing structure, solubility and function."]],
s:["A phospholipid has glycerol, two fatty acids and a phosphate group.","The phosphate head is hydrophilic; the fatty acid tails are hydrophobic.","In water, phospholipids form a bilayer with heads facing outwards and tails inwards.","Triglycerides are energy stores; phospholipids form membranes."]},
"2.9":{c:["Lesson 2.8 Phospholipids","Membranes contain proteins as well as lipids. Proteins do almost every job in a cell. What makes them so versatile?","1. Which part of a phospholipid is hydrophobic?<br>2. Name the monomers of proteins.<br>3. Predict: how many different amino acids are used to make proteins?",3],
h:["One change in one amino acid of haemoglobin causes sickle cell disease. How can such a tiny change matter so much?","fig:AL_23_03","Protein function depends on shape. The sequence of amino acids (primary structure) determines how the chain folds (secondary, tertiary, quaternary structures). In sickle cell haemoglobin, glutamic acid is replaced by valine, which makes molecules stick together at low oxygen, deforming red blood cells."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: building a protein",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the protein simulation.","Build a short chain and watch the peptide bonds form.","Change one amino acid and see the effect on folding.","Record which bonds hold each level of structure."],{sim:"protein"}],
 ["model","Model it: fold a bead chain",12,"String or a long strip of paper; markers",["Each group makes a chain of 12 ‘amino acids’ (marks on the strip), with different R groups written on them.","Twist part into a helix and fold part into a pleated sheet.","Fold the whole chain into a 3D shape, putting ‘hydrophobic’ R groups inside.","Two groups join their chains: quaternary structure.","Label the bonds holding each level."]]],
p:[["What are amino acids?","sketch",2,"Draw the general structure of an amino acid and label the amine, carboxyl and R groups."],
 ["How is a peptide bond formed?","sketch",2,"Show two amino acids joining by condensation and circle the peptide bond."],
 ["What are the four levels of protein structure?","list",2,"Name the four levels and describe each in a few words."],
 ["What is primary structure?","think",1,"Why does primary structure determine all the other levels?"],
 ["What is secondary structure?","comp",2,"Compare the α-helix and β-pleated sheet and name the bond that holds them."],
 ["What is tertiary structure?","list",2,"Name four types of bonds or interactions that hold tertiary structure."],
 ["What is quaternary structure?","think",1,"Give one example of a protein with quaternary structure."],
 ["Which bonds are at each level?","sort",2,"Complete a table: level of structure → bonds involved."]],
s:["Amino acids have an amine group, a carboxyl group and an R group.","Peptide bonds form by condensation between amino acids.","Primary structure is the sequence of amino acids; secondary structure (α-helix, β-pleated sheet) is held by hydrogen bonds.","Tertiary structure is the 3D folding held by hydrogen, ionic, disulfide bonds and hydrophobic interactions; quaternary structure is two or more chains together."]},
"2.10":{c:["Lesson 2.9 Protein structure","Proteins fold into 3D shapes. Some are round and soluble, others long and tough. What decides which?","1. Name the bond that holds secondary structure.<br>2. What is tertiary structure?<br>3. Predict: why does cooking an egg make it go white and solid?",3],
h:["Why does egg white turn from clear runny liquid to a white solid when you fry it?","emo:🍳|Clear and runny, then white and solid.","Heat breaks the hydrogen bonds and other interactions that hold the tertiary structure of albumin, a globular protein. The chains unfold and tangle together (denaturation), so the protein becomes insoluble and white."],
a:[["cards","Card activity: globular or fibrous?",12,"Printed cards (1 set per group)",["Lay out the two heading cards: globular, fibrous.","Sort the feature and example cards.","For each example, link one feature to its function.","Check with another group."],{cards:[["Roughly spherical shape","Globular"],["Soluble in water","Globular"],["Hydrophobic R groups inside","Globular"],["Haemoglobin","Globular"],["Enzymes","Globular"],["Long strands","Fibrous"],["Insoluble in water","Fibrous"],["Repeating sequence of amino acids","Fibrous"],["Collagen","Fibrous"],["Keratin","Fibrous"]],cats:["Globular","Fibrous"]}],
 ["disc","Discussion: perms and hair straighteners",8,"None",["Hairdressers can permanently curl hair by breaking and remaking disulfide bonds in keratin.","Pairs explain why a heat straightener only lasts until hair gets wet.","Share using hydrogen bonds and disulfide bonds."]]],
p:[["How do interactions between R groups shape a protein?","think",1,"Explain why R groups decide how a protein folds."],
 ["What bonds hold a tertiary structure?","comp",2,"Rank the bonds in tertiary structure from strongest to weakest and say what breaks each."],
 ["What are globular proteins like?","list",2,"Give three features of globular proteins and two examples."],
 ["What are fibrous proteins like?","list",2,"Give three features of fibrous proteins and two examples."],
 ["How do globular and fibrous proteins compare?","comp",2,"Make a table comparing shape, solubility, sequence and function."]],
s:["R groups interact to fold the chain into its tertiary structure.","Bonds: hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bonds and hydrophobic interactions.","Globular proteins are spherical and soluble, with hydrophobic R groups inside (enzymes, haemoglobin).","Fibrous proteins are long, insoluble and strong, often with repeating sequences (collagen, keratin)."]},
"2.11":{c:["Lesson 2.10 Globular and fibrous proteins","Haemoglobin is globular; collagen is fibrous. How does each structure fit its job?","1. Give one feature of a globular protein.<br>2. Name a fibrous protein.<br>3. Predict: why must haemoglobin be soluble?",3],
h:["Why does a carbon monoxide leak from a charcoal brazier in a closed room kill people?","emo:🔥 🚪 ⚠️|A charcoal brazier in a closed room overnight.","Carbon monoxide binds to the haem groups of haemoglobin far more strongly than oxygen does, forming carboxyhaemoglobin. Less oxygen is carried to the tissues, so cells cannot respire aerobically."],
a:[["case","Case study: charcoal braziers and carbon monoxide",12,"Board only",["Read the case on the board.","Q1. Which part of haemoglobin does carbon monoxide bind to?","Q2. Why are symptoms worse in a closed room?","Q3. Why is the patient given pure oxygen?","Q4. Write one safety message for families."],{study:"In the cold season, some families bring a charcoal brazier indoors to keep warm at night. Burning charcoal in a small closed room uses up oxygen and produces carbon monoxide, a colourless, odourless gas. Hospitals report cases of headache, dizziness, unconsciousness and sometimes deaths. Patients are treated with high concentrations of oxygen.",src:"Based on Zambian news reports and WHO guidance on carbon monoxide poisoning",ans:["The iron in the haem group.","Carbon monoxide builds up because there is no ventilation.","High oxygen concentration helps displace carbon monoxide from haemoglobin.","Never use a brazier in a closed room; keep windows open; put the fire out before sleeping."]}],
 ["disc","Discussion: collagen everywhere",8,"None",["Collagen is in skin, tendons, bone and blood vessel walls.","Pairs list two features of collagen and the tissue where each matters most.","Share: why is vitamin C needed to make collagen (link to scurvy)?"]]],
p:[["What is the structure of haemoglobin?","label",2,"Sketch haemoglobin: four polypeptide chains, four haem groups. Label α and β chains."],
 ["How does haemoglobin’s structure suit its function?","expl",2,"Explain how the haem group and the soluble globular shape help haemoglobin carry oxygen."],
 ["Where is collagen found?","list",1,"List four places in the body where collagen is found."],
 ["What is the structure of collagen?","sort",2,"Put in order: primary chain with glycine every third amino acid, helix, three chains form triple helix, fibrils, fibres."],
 ["How does collagen’s structure suit its function?","expl",2,"Explain why collagen has high tensile strength."],
 ["How do collagen and haemoglobin compare?","comp",2,"Compare collagen and haemoglobin in a table."]],
s:["Haemoglobin is a globular protein with four polypeptide chains, each with a haem group containing iron (Fe²⁺) that binds oxygen.","Its hydrophilic outer R groups make it soluble in plasma.","Collagen is a fibrous protein: three chains in a triple helix with glycine every third amino acid, cross-linked into fibrils and fibres.","Collagen gives tensile strength to skin, tendons, bone and artery walls."]},
"2.12":{c:["Lesson 2.11 Haemoglobin and collagen","Haemoglobin dissolves in plasma, which is mostly water. Why is water such a good solvent?","1. Name the protein that carries oxygen.<br>2. Recall: what is a hydrogen bond?<br>3. Predict: why does ice float on water?",3],
h:["Why can a pond skater walk on water, and why does ice float on a pond instead of sinking?","emo:💧 🦟 🧊|An insect standing on water. Ice floating on top.","Water molecules are polar and form hydrogen bonds with each other. This gives high cohesion and surface tension (the insect stays on top), and when water freezes the hydrogen bonds hold molecules further apart, so ice is less dense than liquid water and floats."],
a:[["disc","Discussion: what if water did not hydrogen bond?",10,"None",["Imagine water molecules could not form hydrogen bonds.","Pairs predict three changes to life on Earth.","Share and link each to a property of water."]],
 ["prob","Problem solving: explain the observations",12,"Board only",["Your teacher lists observations: sweating cools you; lakes rarely freeze solid; water climbs up thin glass tubes; salt dissolves in water but oil does not.","For each, name the property of water involved.","Explain each using polarity or hydrogen bonds."]]],
p:[["Why does water matter so much to life?","list",1,"Give three reasons water is essential for life."],
 ["Why is water a polar molecule?","sketch",2,"Draw a water molecule with δ+ and δ− charges."],
 ["How do water molecules form hydrogen bonds?","sketch",2,"Draw three water molecules showing the hydrogen bonds between them."],
 ["Which properties of water come from hydrogen bonding?","list",2,"List four properties of water that result from hydrogen bonds."]],
s:["Water is polar: oxygen is slightly negative and hydrogens slightly positive.","Hydrogen bonds form between the δ+ hydrogen of one molecule and the δ− oxygen of another.","Hydrogen bonding gives water high specific heat capacity, high latent heat of vaporisation, cohesion and surface tension.","Ice is less dense than liquid water."]},
"2.13":{c:["Lesson 2.12 Water and hydrogen bonds","Water’s properties come from hydrogen bonds. How does each property help living organisms?","1. Why is water polar?<br>2. Name two properties caused by hydrogen bonds.<br>3. Predict: why do we sweat when we are hot?",3],
h:["Why does the temperature of Lake Kariba change far less between day and night than the air above it?","emo:🌊 ☀️ 🌙|Air: 18 °C at night, 34 °C by day. Lake surface: almost the same.","Water has a high specific heat capacity: a lot of energy is needed to raise its temperature, because hydrogen bonds must be broken. This makes lakes and cells thermally stable habitats."],
a:[["cards","Card activity: property and role",12,"Printed cards (1 set per group)",["Match each property of water to its biological importance.","For each pair, write the link to hydrogen bonding.","Check with another group."],{cards:[["Good solvent for polar and ionic substances","Transport in blood and xylem; medium for reactions"],["High specific heat capacity","Stable temperatures in cells and lakes"],["High latent heat of vaporisation","Cooling by sweating and transpiration"],["Cohesion between molecules","Water columns in xylem"],["Ice is less dense than water","Ice insulates water below; organisms survive"],["Transparent","Light reaches aquatic plants"]],pairs:1}],
 ["disc","Discussion: sweat on a humid day",8,"None",["Why do you feel hotter on a humid day in November than on a dry day at the same temperature?","Pairs explain using evaporation and latent heat.","Share."]]],
p:[["Why do water’s properties matter to organisms?","think",1,"Choose one property of water and explain why life would be difficult without it."],
 ["Why is water a good solvent?","expl",2,"Explain how water dissolves sodium chloride and glucose."],
 ["Why does a high specific heat capacity help organisms?","expl",2,"Explain why the temperature of cells changes slowly."],
 ["Why does a high latent heat of vaporisation help organisms?","expl",2,"Explain how sweating removes a lot of heat."],
 ["What other properties of water matter?","list",2,"Give two other properties of water and their importance."],
 ["How can we summarise water’s roles?","sort",2,"Complete a summary table: property → cause → importance."]],
s:["Water dissolves polar and ionic substances, so it is the medium for reactions and transport.","High specific heat capacity keeps temperatures stable in organisms and habitats.","High latent heat of vaporisation means evaporation of sweat or transpiration removes much heat.","Cohesion and surface tension help water move in xylem and support small organisms."]}
});
